      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{628d00a6b624bb0b622328ef|full_name} Yea, this might be not obvious, but in Cyrillic languages \"t\" is written as \"m\" in script, while \"m\" as \"м\" both in script and typography.\n\n 🥲 🥲 🥲 \n\nSo, as actual glyph \"m\" in Cyrillic can only be encounted in script and means \"t\", Russian mind reads all the \"m\" as \"t\" automatically.\n\nAnd all physics units (both SI and non-SI) transliterated (except for Celsius for some reason), written Cyrillic. \n\nI remember how in middle school we were rofling on each other that m&m's \"actually\" is t&t's 🤡\n\nIt's not big deal, most of users sure be able to figure out what this means, but when entire UI is translated, those **small details became catchy** and inconsistent.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
